A||N||5||fastback87||Alignment on a Fiero (some different stories)||||5||
Z||000000||fastback87||10-31-2001||01:39 PM||||I accidentally posted this in THE MALL, Sorry.<P>Does anyone know of any special requirements when doing an alignment on a fiero that are unique to this type of car or similar to BMW and Mercedes???<BR>I was at my local alignment shop and a guy that used to live in AZ told me that 10 (or more) years ago they used to have to add wieght to the front seats and then do an alignment or it would be off bad. I just want to know if anyone else has heard this??<P>The next place I went told me that it had a celebrity frontend in the rear and a chevette frontend in the front??? He said why would there be anything special to an alignment, "It's just a Fiero" (d!ck!!). I think that is BS but what do I know?!?!<P>I would just like to know if anyone has every heard of this and if they could point me somewhere that might be able to tell me...<P>Thanks guy's I love this forum.<P>James<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||10-31-2001||02:14 PM||||That's alright.. I accidently replied over there.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Never heard of the weights in the seats???<P>The rear is different from most cars because it has independent rear suspension with adjustable camber (wheel leaning in or out) and toe in (how far from parallel the two wheels are). This is probably the reference to BMW and Mercedes. <P>Alignment should not be particularly hard for anyone that knows what they are doing though. A little more to adjust but not really THAT different.<P>Oh, the other guy is right at least for 84-87 Fieros. The front end is very similar to a Chevette and the rear end is basically the front end from a GM front wheel drive from the 80s (Although I think it's closer to a Citation than a Celebrity). They just connected the tie rods to the frame instead of a steering rack.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||starfighter007@msn.com||10-31-2001||05:23 PM||||What they were telling you about the weight in therory is correct but not nessary I would say that it was a over zelous tech. talking.all the specs that they publish for front end shops take the drivers weight approx. into account and the crown in the road into account.Most importantantly you need to find a good shop that will do a good alignment that in my opinon is the hardest part of getting a alignment.Talk to some body shops in your area and find out where they send there cars.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||10-31-2001||09:44 PM||||Fiero alignments are slightly different from most other cars.  <BR>In the front end, the toe is adjusted the same as any other car.  The caster is adjusted by adding or removing washers that place the fore and aft positioning of the upper control arm in its pivot bolt, thus moving the fore and aft position of the upper ball joint.  The camber is non-adjustable, but sometimes they can drill out the rivets tha hold it in place and rotate it 180 degrees, all at an extra cost to you (cheapest I saw was $20 a side ten years ago).  Te best way to make the front camber right is to replace the upper balljoints with Moog Problem Solvers, which have a long slot where the bolts go, which gives plenty of adjustability to the camber, even if you lower it.  Most cars with short/long arm type front ends use shims on the upper control arm pivot rod to move the entire control arm in or out, but the 84-87 Fieros (and Chevettes) don't have this ability (even though the alignment machine may tell the "technician" to do so).  The 88's do have this ability.  The good news is that the Moog Problem Solvers are cheap if you shop around, but the shop probably won't install them unless you buy them there, usually at a much higher cost than you can get them yourself.  Mine cost me $20 a piece back in '95, while a certain "Fiero specialty store" wanted more than double that.  All you need to install them are a drill, a jack and stands, a torque wrench and hand tools, and a pickle fork.  About an hour per side if you have any clue about tools.  Alignment shops hate them because they mean more work than just setting the toe for their $60 alignment price (the front wheels have to come off to make any camber adjustments), so they may try to flat-rate the cost by the hour.<P>The rear end is really easy.  The caster isn't adjusted, the camber is easy with aftermarket struts, since the lower bolt hole in the strut is slotted to allow the knuckle to be pivoted in or out on the strut.  There are also camber kits (special bolts) that are reasonably priced to allow the same thing.  Never needed them myself, but I hear they are convenient.  Toe is set the same as the front end.<P>As far as weights go, mine makes no difference with or without.  With the poly bushings and lowering springs, my ride height doesn't change enough to matter.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||11-01-2001||10:35 PM||||That's cool if you found a shop that's fussy enough to add weight,as it'll just make for a better alignment.I heard of a case where a very large person was having some problems with their car in terms of alignment/driveability,finally wound up sitting in the car while it was being adjusted.<BR>Most shops usually quit at the caster part,if not sooner.Always get the print-out.<BR>I'm usually relieved when they say"it's just a Fiero",lots of places/people freak out;"whhooa,uh,ieea don't know,uuh,it might be tough".||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||11-02-2001||10:19 AM||||If a shop doing any work for me won't let me be involved at all, such as listeing to what I want done and getting me a real printout, I'll walk.  Luckily for me, the Navy was kind enough to spend the cash on a Hunter rack for the auto hobby shop on the base.  $5 an hour to DIY, with some assistance from the guys that run the shop.  I just did a "perfect" setup on my 97 T-bird in under 2 hours.  I'll do the Fiero and Mustang before I move again, for sure.  One of the simplest jobs I've ever done, too.||||reg||1
